one of several deadly shootings we've  reported on in the last week the rise in  violence has funeral directors and  morticians speaking out reminding the  community about the impact these  incidents have madison kimbrough shares  their message tonight  the stop the violence march started  around 3 30 this afternoon and was meant  to bring awareness to the influx of  crime that's been happening here on the  west side and also across the valley  local funeral homes slowly processioned  through with empty hearses and families  who had lost loved ones to violence  carried signs and posters with their  family members pictures and the chant  was simple stop the violence increase  the peace about 100 people participated  on foot also driving by honking their  horns and coming together for one common  cause funeral director dr raymond lewis  giddens junior says they've been  planning this event for four months now  and are pleased to see it through we  want to just encourage the community to  be aware of issues that take place day  in and day out where young people older  people are dying from senseless violence  in the community metro assisted in the  march to make sure that traffic ran  smoothly and a reception took place at  true love missionary baptist church to  honor the lives that were lost  and the march that originally started on  owens avenue happened in the same area  where a homicide took place just a  couple days ago the organizers say they  want to open up a funeral home in that  same area sometime next year reporting  in the historic west side madison  kimbrough live local now
 

